"q-130313" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12caa388-0d74-555f-a2e4-090f1c84bced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293654} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2c1fee-2568-529b-99c1-a0c082fe9d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293655} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e50a0e-db8c-55f3-84dc-49e8eb1c1375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293656} {:answer/id #uuid "0512ff9e-d02a-5795-a20a-a2c0aee0d6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293657} {:answer/id #uuid "5a134db0-33ff-5af3-90fc-739c59a88ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee2c1fee-2568-529b-99c1-a0c082fe9d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293655} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8308f41-f531-5896-96da-f280b054d33f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbc4b4ed-d2ff-5bf8-96ba-ad5cf7b8d58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130446" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- ${A}^{2}B=A(AB)$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $B(CA)=(CB)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  ${A}^{2}B=AAB=A(AB)$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false.  Matrix multiplication is distributive and  $A(B-C)=AB-AC.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AC\\ne CA$ in general and therefore $A(B-C)\\ne AB-CA$, in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $B(CA)=(BC)A.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general and therefore $B(CA)\\ne (CB)A$, in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cbbffd2-c482-515d-8bcc-7e95b417ee3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cbbffd2-c482-515d-8bcc-7e95b417ee3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293661} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6da346-ec1e-523a-acd9-74dfb0bb9e66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289107a9-561a-5bdd-9f05-49dfec32a3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "289107a9-561a-5bdd-9f05-49dfec32a3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293663} :answer-field/id #uuid "76592d76-ccfa-56a1-9df0-9382c809d481" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e617169-2a55-53ef-9dc0-d411029e0141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e617169-2a55-53ef-9dc0-d411029e0141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293665} :answer-field/id #uuid "e953a819-9007-5b5b-ac21-e054240b488e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "155f2f4f-9651-51d7-980e-63b64b381dae" :question/math-academy-id "q-319938" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-65 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9d8b90c-5146-5c84-973e-4bd38ba78f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}55 \\\\ -21 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293668} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6ce2a0-1b29-5a69-a9e4-19331922dd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293669} {:answer/id #uuid "0882e4e8-1726-54c1-9119-098c961e86f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293670} {:answer/id #uuid "48642b3a-eed1-5040-8676-f0057b1bb962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293671} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6b1385-dbb8-5d1d-8c8e-6dac38da050e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd6ce2a0-1b29-5a69-a9e4-19331922dd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293669} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c81560-207d-5da8-a1c2-842d1cbf8404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cbd1e16-e623-56fd-8563-e7c6cd4b5ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42258" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c36233db-f41a-5199-a3f7-98523b3a0156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113860} {:answer/id #uuid "04a98c87-0c82-54c1-b470-2a5844f5ad25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113861} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3b4c8e-1177-5bd2-bf47-d815ae29ca50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113862} {:answer/id #uuid "283cbb6a-032b-5896-ab88-22f67dfb1530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113863} {:answer/id #uuid "41ff8964-5862-5158-92d0-7266420853ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a98c87-0c82-54c1-b470-2a5844f5ad25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113861} :answer-field/id #uuid "efcaf131-c330-56fb-ac84-73e6920c535f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e648555-87e2-52bf-ac70-24ae7f7d32c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42265" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."}]}
